WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.492
Take one map of the plane

2
00:00:05.492 --> 00:00:09.180
projection onto the line y equals x.

3
00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:11.007
The point three

4
00:00:11.007 --> 00:00:14.882
one drops straight onto the line, at two

5
00:00:14.882 --> 00:00:15.382
two.

6
00:00:15.436 --> 00:00:18.389
In the standard basis the matrix

7
00:00:18.481 --> 00:00:21.250
of this map is all one halves.

8
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.591
Is there a better basis

9
00:00:23.591 --> 00:00:26.911
one where this map has a simpler table?

10
00:00:26.911 --> 00:00:29.400
Pause and choose two vectors.

11
00:00:33.694 --> 00:00:36.767
Choose the basis that suits the map.

12
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:40.437
Lay down a new grid, with v one, one, one

13
00:00:40.437 --> 00:00:44.107
along the line, and v two, one, minus one

14
00:00:44.107 --> 00:00:45.814
straight across it.

15
00:00:46.164 --> 00:00:48.404
The projection keeps v one

16
00:00:48.404 --> 00:00:50.643
it is already on the line.

17
00:00:50.643 --> 00:00:54.044
And it kills v two, which drops to zero.

18
00:00:54.394 --> 00:00:59.571
So in this basis the matrix is one, zero

19
00:00:59.571 --> 00:01:01.086
zero, zero.

20
00:01:01.086 --> 00:01:04.874
Same map, much simpler table.

21
00:01:05.454 --> 00:01:08.155
How are the two tables related?

22
00:01:08.155 --> 00:01:11.467
Follow one point through three steps.

23
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:12.948
In the new grid

24
00:01:13.035 --> 00:01:15.475
the point is two steps along

25
00:01:15.563 --> 00:01:17.828
v one and one step across

26
00:01:17.828 --> 00:01:20.094
new coordinates two, one.

27
00:01:20.444 --> 00:01:25.724
First B turns new coordinates into old ones

28
00:01:25.724 --> 00:01:30.044
two v one plus v two is three, one.

29
00:01:30.394 --> 00:01:32.381
Then A does the projection

30
00:01:32.457 --> 00:01:35.514
in old coordinates, landing at two, two.

31
00:01:35.864 --> 00:01:36.383
Last

32
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:40.951
B inverse reads the answer in the new grid

33
00:01:40.951 --> 00:01:44.481
two v one, coordinates two, zero.

34
00:01:44.481 --> 00:01:47.076
Read from right to left

35
00:01:47.076 --> 00:01:49.464
that is B inverse A B.

36
00:01:49.814 --> 00:01:53.892
Here the new basis is made of eigenvectors

37
00:01:53.987 --> 00:01:57.021
so B inverse A B is the diagonal

38
00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:59.203
matrix of eigenvalues.

39
00:01:59.297 --> 00:02:01.953
Diagonalizing is a change to

40
00:02:02.048 --> 00:02:04.134
the eigenvector basis.

41
00:02:04.714 --> 00:02:08.651
Two matrices are similar when one equals

42
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.097
M inverse times the other times M.

43
00:02:12.097 --> 00:02:16.034
They are one map, written in two bases.

44
00:02:16.384 --> 00:02:20.074
Lecture eighteen's matrix four, one, two

45
00:02:20.074 --> 00:02:20.704
three.

46
00:02:20.704 --> 00:02:21.694
As a warp

47
00:02:21.784 --> 00:02:25.024
it stretches the blue eigenvector by

48
00:02:25.114 --> 00:02:27.904
five and the orange one by two.

49
00:02:28.254 --> 00:02:32.265
With M equal to the shear one, one, zero

50
00:02:32.265 --> 00:02:36.471
one, it becomes the triangular matrix two

51
00:02:36.471 --> 00:02:38.134
zero, two, five.

52
00:02:38.484 --> 00:02:41.411
Same map, so the same eigenvalues

53
00:02:41.411 --> 00:02:44.424
five and two, now on the diagonal.

54
00:02:44.424 --> 00:02:48.298
The same trace, seven, the same determinant

55
00:02:48.298 --> 00:02:50.364
ten, and the same rank.

56
00:02:50.714 --> 00:02:53.564
What changes are the coordinates.

57
00:02:53.564 --> 00:02:57.365
In the grid of M, the blue eigenvector one

58
00:02:57.365 --> 00:03:00.043
one is described as zero, one.

59
00:03:00.043 --> 00:03:02.634
One vector, two descriptions.

60
00:03:03.214 --> 00:03:06.947
So it is tempting to turn this around

61
00:03:06.947 --> 00:03:09.894
same eigenvalues, so similar?

62
00:03:10.244 --> 00:03:14.126
The identity and the shear one, one, zero

63
00:03:14.126 --> 00:03:17.731
one both have eigenvalues one and one

64
00:03:17.731 --> 00:03:21.244
the same trace, the same determinant.

65
00:03:21.594 --> 00:03:24.517
But the identity does nothing

66
00:03:24.517 --> 00:03:27.246
in every grid you lay down

67
00:03:27.246 --> 00:03:30.364
M inverse times I times M is I.

68
00:03:30.364 --> 00:03:34.554
Nothing but the identity is similar to it.

69
00:03:34.904 --> 00:03:37.619
The shear really moves the plane

70
00:03:37.702 --> 00:03:39.923
and only one line of arrows

71
00:03:40.006 --> 00:03:41.651
keeps its direction.

72
00:03:41.651 --> 00:03:43.544
It is a different map.

73
00:03:44.124 --> 00:03:46.738
So some maps cannot be made

74
00:03:46.834 --> 00:03:48.964
diagonal in any basis.

75
00:03:48.964 --> 00:03:52.061
The derivative on P two is one

76
00:03:52.061 --> 00:03:54.868
its only eigenvalue is zero

77
00:03:54.868 --> 00:03:59.224
and its only eigenvectors are the constants.

78
00:03:59.224 --> 00:04:01.644
One line is not a basis.

79
00:04:01.994 --> 00:04:04.364
The best basis is a chain

80
00:04:04.364 --> 00:04:07.828
one half t squared, then t, then one.

81
00:04:07.828 --> 00:04:11.748
The derivative moves each one to the next

82
00:04:11.748 --> 00:04:13.754
and one goes to zero.

83
00:04:14.104 --> 00:04:16.521
So the matrix has ones just

84
00:04:16.611 --> 00:04:19.744
above the diagonal: a Jordan block.

85
00:04:19.744 --> 00:04:23.504
The shear is the two by two Jordan block

86
00:04:23.504 --> 00:04:25.384
with eigenvalue one.

87
00:04:25.964 --> 00:04:28.001
Change of basis also happens

88
00:04:28.074 --> 00:04:29.529
in machine learning.

89
00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:32.075
A linear model prices a house with

90
00:04:32.148 --> 00:04:33.966
weights three and twenty

91
00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:36.658
eighty square metres and three rooms

92
00:04:36.731 --> 00:04:39.204
give three hundred thousand euros.

93
00:04:39.554 --> 00:04:41.616
Measure the area in tens of

94
00:04:41.692 --> 00:04:43.372
square metres instead.

95
00:04:43.372 --> 00:04:45.663
To keep the same predictions

96
00:04:45.663 --> 00:04:48.259
the weights change the other way

97
00:04:48.259 --> 00:04:49.710
thirty and twenty.

98
00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.314
Still three hundred.

99
00:04:51.664 --> 00:04:53.410
The model is the same

100
00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.187
but training sees the coordinates.

101
00:04:56.187 --> 00:04:59.123
Here is one level curve of the loss

102
00:04:59.123 --> 00:05:01.424
so thin it is almost a line.

103
00:05:01.774 --> 00:05:04.720
Change the weights with the features

104
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.029
and the same curve opens up.

105
00:05:07.109 --> 00:05:09.896
The condition number of the Hessian

106
00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:12.921
drops from about one hundred nineteen

107
00:05:13.001 --> 00:05:15.231
thousand to sixteen hundred

108
00:05:15.231 --> 00:05:18.734
and gradient descent needs far fewer steps.

109
00:05:19.314 --> 00:05:23.137
So: one map in two bases gives

110
00:05:23.264 --> 00:05:27.851
two similar matrices, B inverse A B

111
00:05:27.851 --> 00:05:31.674
convert, apply, convert back.

112
00:05:32.024 --> 00:05:34.581
They share eigenvalues, trace

113
00:05:34.581 --> 00:05:36.455
determinant and rank.

114
00:05:36.455 --> 00:05:39.864
The best basis is made of eigenvectors

115
00:05:39.864 --> 00:05:42.250
and when there are too few

116
00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:43.784
of Jordan chains.

117
00:05:44.134 --> 00:05:47.370
Next: the singular value decomposition

118
00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.943
two good bases for any matrix

119
00:05:49.943 --> 00:05:51.934
even a rectangular one.
